The community hall sends you two versions of its Saturday schedule. You
want to compare them without editing either one. A colleague suggests
\cmd|cmp -s| and says a quiet screen means the schedules agree. But the
screen is just as quiet when you mistype a filename. To do this job reliably
you need to know what you asked the command to do and how it tells you what
happened. Those are separate questions.

\begin{objectives}
\item Separate the command name, options, option values, and operands.
\item Pass a name with spaces or an initial hyphen as one literal argument.
\item Read a utility's exit status and distinguish a negative answer from an error.
\end{objectives}

\section{Start with two drafts}
\label{sec:13-drafts}
The examples use POSIX-style \texttt{sh}. If your everyday shell uses
different syntax, type \cmd|sh| to start a practice shell, and type
\cmd|exit| at its prompt when you finish. The unadorned commands in the
input panels are what you type; the terminal's prompt is not part of them.
This chapter doesn't need any of your own documents.

\begin{terminal}
lab=$(mktemp -d) && cd "$lab" && pwd
\end{terminal}

\cmd|mktemp -d| makes a new private directory on the platforms covered
here, although it is not a POSIX utility. The \cmd|$(...)| captures the
printed path and \cmd|lab=| remembers it; the double quotes preserve the
path as one argument even if it has spaces. Each \cmd|&&| runs the next
step only if the preceding one succeeds. If the line fails or doesn't
print the new path, stop. Record the path shown by \cmd|pwd|. If you
close the practice shell prematurely, the variable won't be there when
you return, but the recorded path will still help you identify the lab.

\begin{terminal}
printf '%s\n' 'Doors noon' 'Tea at one' > schedule.txt
cp schedule.txt 'Saturday draft.txt'
printf '%s\n' 'Doors noon' 'Tea at two' > ./-revised.txt
\end{terminal}

Only run those lines in the new directory. The \cmd|>| writes the
printed text into the named file, replacing contents if it already
exists; Chapter~\ref{ch:16} explains this operation in detail. In
\cmd|printf| the quoted format \cmd|%s\n| means print a string followed
by a newline. It repeats for the two supplied strings. The quotes keep
each phrase together. The copy has the original text; the third file
differs only in the time for tea. These small, known differences give
you something to check against your reading of the commands.

\begin{terminal}
cmp schedule.txt 'Saturday draft.txt'
cmp schedule.txt ./-revised.txt
\end{terminal}

The first command normally says nothing: the two inputs match
byte-for-byte. The second normally names the files and identifies the
first differing position. Its exact wording varies with the utility;
in this fixture the difference is on line two. That is a useful result,
not a broken command. Conversely, \cmd|cmp| does not know whether what
you meant by ``same'' was ``the same event times'' despite changes to
formatting. It can answer only the question specified by its manual.

\section{Read from the outside in}
\label{sec:13-arguments}
\IndexTerm{argument}
\IndexTerm{option}
\IndexTerm{operand}
\IndexCommand{cmp}
\IndexCommand{printf}
In \cmd|cmp -s schedule.txt 'Saturday draft.txt'|, read \cmd|cmp| as
the name of the command, \cmd|-s| as the instruction to be silent,
and the two paths as the things to compare. An \emph{argument} is an
individual value the shell passes after the command name. An
\emph{option} selects a behavior and an \emph{operand} supplies data
or a target; both are passed as arguments. Some options take a value
of their own. The shell doesn't consult the \cmd|cmp| manual and
attach labels to the arguments. It gets the arguments ready; the
command decides what they mean. Figure~\ref{fig:13-arguments} shows
the difference.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering\input{figures/chapter-13/arguments.tikz}
\caption{The shell hands over three arguments; \texttt{cmp} gives each a role.}
\label{fig:13-arguments}
\VisualDescription{The silent option, first path, and quoted second path occupy three separate argument positions. The second path remains one argument.}
\end{figure}

The blank between \cmd|Saturday| and \cmd|draft.txt| is part of the
second path. The quotes themselves are shell notation, so \cmd|cmp|
doesn't receive those. If you omit the quotes, you don't merely
change the spelling of an operand: you supply an additional argument.
Read the following \cmd|printf| call as a little inspection tool.
It prints each data argument surrounded by square brackets, one per
line. Those brackets are part of the format and not part of the names.

\begin{terminal}
printf '[%s]\n' schedule.txt 'Saturday draft.txt'
printf '[%s]\n' schedule.txt Saturday draft.txt
\end{terminal}

Expect two bracketed lines in the former case, three in the latter.
That remains a useful test when the intended command is more
complicated. Once the shell has split an unquoted name into two
arguments, a utility cannot always tell those apart from two separate
names that you really meant to provide. Joining the words with quotes
in the input is clearer than relying on a receiving program to guess.

\begin{terminal}
ls -l -d .
ls -ld .
head -n 1 'Saturday draft.txt'
\end{terminal}

\cmd|ls -l| requests a long listing, and \cmd|-d| asks for information
about the directory named by the operand \cmd|.| rather than a listing
of its contents. The detailed fields aren't needed here: check the final
name. For these \cmd|ls| switches, \cmd|-ld| expresses the same request.
\cmd|head -n 1| instead has an option \cmd|-n| that needs the argument
\cmd|1| to supply a count; the path is still a different argument.
Expect \texttt{Doors noon}. The roles are fixed by the \cmd|head|
manual, not merely by where a word appears on the screen. When in
doubt, prefer the spaced form documented in the synopsis and keep
options ahead of operands. Some utilities accept interleaved options
and operands; don't count on that habit traveling from your machine.

\begin{quicktable}{M{1.4in}Y}
\toprule
Example fragment & What to look up \\
\midrule
cmp -s & Whether silence changes printing, status, or both. \\
head -n 1 & The unit and allowed values of the count. \\
ls -ld . & Whether the two switches can be grouped; what dot names. \\
printf format data & The first operand is a format and the remaining operands are data. \\
\bottomrule
\end{quicktable}

A synopsis commonly uses brackets to show optional material and
ellipsis to show repetition. Neither is automatically something to
type. Chapter~\ref{ch:10} explains how to find the manual for the
utility you are actually running. Many GNU utilities offer long options
starting with two hyphens, but portable examples should use the
documented POSIX options where possible. Some utility names also name
a shell builtin: for example \cmd|printf| may run inside the shell,
even when a separate system executable exists. If you get surprising
behavior, first identify what is running; requesting \cmd|--help| is
not a universal substitute for reading the appropriate manual.

\begin{terminal}
command -V printf
command -V cmp
\end{terminal}

\cmd|command -V| asks the shell to describe how it resolves the supplied
name. The descriptions depend on the shell and your configuration.
Compare the two descriptions rather than expecting a particular path.
It's better to know which version of a command you're addressing before
asking why a flag works on a colleague's machine but not on yours.

\section{A hyphen can belong to a name}
\label{sec:13-hyphen}
\IndexTerm{leading hyphen}
Look back at the third filename. Suppose an attachment arrives named
\texttt{-revised.txt}. Writing quotes around this name preserves it as
one argument, but it was already one argument: there was no space in
it. The quotes don't tell the utility how to interpret it. After the
shell removes them, the receiving program gets an argument beginning
with \texttt{-} and may try to read it as an option. A portable way
to pass a relative filename in the current directory is to spell it
with a \texttt{./} prefix. The operating system locates the same file;
the utility sees an argument that starts with a dot, not a hyphen.

\begin{terminal}
head -n 1 ./-revised.txt
head -n 1 -- -revised.txt
\end{terminal}

Both requests print \texttt{Doors noon} with a conforming \cmd|head|.
For utilities that recognize it, \cmd|--| ends option processing, so
the following hyphen belongs to an operand. Don't put it in every
command by reflex: consult the applicable manual or use the path form.
In some utilities \cmd|-| alone is a special operand, often referring
to standard input; naming the actual file as \cmd|./-| avoids assigning
it that special meaning. Figure~\ref{fig:13-hyphen} puts the two layers
side by side.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering\input{figures/chapter-13/hyphen.tikz}
\caption{The prefix changes the argument's first character, not the file it names.}
\label{fig:13-hyphen}
\VisualDescription{Both spellings name the same file, but only the spelling with dot slash starts with a dot. Quotes alone leave the initial hyphen untouched.}
\end{figure}

Treat an odd filename as a good reason to test a read-only command
first. For example, if you intend to file an attachment away under a
new, clearer name, inspect it by its exact path before you consider
moving it. You can also use an absolute path you already confirmed.
Don't type an option from an error message just to make the message
go away: if a filename was mistaken for an option, the answer is to
repair the way you pass the filename. This matters particularly for
operations that write, move, or delete things.

\section{The answer you cannot see}
\label{sec:13-status}
\IndexTerm{exit status}
The shell receives a number when a command finishes, independently of
what the command prints. That number is its \emph{exit status}. Zero
usually marks success. The exact meaning of a nonzero value is part of
the command's contract: with \cmd|cmp|, a nonzero result can be a
legitimate answer. Read \cmd|$?| as ``the previous status in this shell.''
The double quotes preserve the value as one argument. Run the
\cmd|printf| immediately after the command you want to check, without
running an extra command in between. Even an innocent \cmd|pwd| will
replace the status before you can look.

\begin{terminal}
cmp -s schedule.txt 'Saturday draft.txt'
printf 'Status: %s\n' "$?"
cmp -s schedule.txt ./-revised.txt
printf 'Status: %s\n' "$?"
cmp -s schedule.txt not-created.txt
printf 'Status: %s\n' "$?"
\end{terminal}

The first status is zero; the second is one; the third must be above
one for a conforming \cmd|cmp|. A diagnostic for the third command
is allowed even with \cmd|-s|; its presence and wording are not the
test. The former two conditions are answers after a comparison. The
third means the comparison couldn't give you either answer. A
terminal full of text does not itself prove a problem, either: plain
\cmd|cmp| can describe the first difference as part of doing its job.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering\input{figures/chapter-13/status.tikz}
\caption{Read a status relative to the question the command answers.}
\label{fig:13-status}
\VisualDescription{A comparison can say equal with zero, different with one, or not completed due to trouble with a status above one.}
\end{figure}

\begin{quicktable}{M{1.4in}Y}
\toprule
Status from cmp & Next decision \\
\midrule
0 & Contents match. Decide separately whether that suffices to replace either file. \\
1 & Contents differ. Inspect the difference; don't infer which is newer. \\
Greater than 1 & Check the filenames, accessibility, and diagnostic before taking any action. \\
\bottomrule
\end{quicktable}

For another command, look up its own status rules instead of copying
this table. The shell commonly reports status 127 if it cannot find
the command and 126 if it finds the command to execute but cannot
execute it. Those are not numbers returned by \cmd|cmp| after
comparing your files. Read the message and name involved. In a command
line with more than one command, make yourself ask \emph{which} result
you are seeing. The right result for \cmd|pwd| tells you nothing about
the left result for \cmd|cmp|. Chapter~\ref{ch:17} extends this
distinction to pipelines, where the stage you see least may be the one
that failed first.

\section{Make an ambiguous instruction checkable}
\label{sec:13-check}
Imagine you get a message that says ``Use the latest schedule.''
Nothing in \cmd|cmp| orders the files by date or tells you who
approved them. A difference only proves that the files don't have
the same bytes. On the other hand, if the task was to check that a
copy landed intact, zero is evidence about the copy's bytes.
The same tool gives a good answer to one request and an answer
that needs more context for the other. Get as far as phrasing
the question before choosing the options.

Suppose you want the status for your own notes but want to
type \cmd|pwd| before displaying it. Save the status right
away. There is no blank on either side of the equals sign in
the assignment; it stores data in the current shell rather
than sending arguments to a command. Chapter~\ref{ch:27}
develops variables beyond this small use.

\begin{terminal}
cmp -s schedule.txt ./-revised.txt
comparison=$?
pwd
printf 'Saved comparison status: %s\n' "$comparison"
\end{terminal}

The path and the one now report different events. Typing
\cmd|printf '%s\n' "$?"| on the last line instead would ask for
the status of \cmd|pwd| and normally print zero. There wasn't
a change in either schedule. You simply changed which question
the printout answers. If you attach the result to an email,
write ``the two schedules differ'' rather than ``the
comparison failed.'' The former tells the recipient what
they need to investigate.

Taking arguments apart can help with another ambiguous
message: a colleague sends a command containing
\cmd|head -n '2 Saturday draft.txt'|. You could spend a while
looking for an invisible punctuation error in the filename.
Instead, read what the utility receives: after \cmd|-n|
there is one argument, \texttt{2 Saturday draft.txt}. It isn't
a line count. Nothing will be left over as a file operand.
Compare that to two separate arguments, one for the number
and one for the name.

\begin{terminal}
printf '<%s>\n' -n '2 Saturday draft.txt'
printf '<%s>\n' -n 2 'Saturday draft.txt'
\end{terminal}

The angle brackets belong only to \cmd|printf|'s display
format. Compare the second reported argument from each
command. This test doesn't simulate \cmd|head|; it makes the
boundary mistake apparent without starting it. Keep the
placeholder \cmd|%s| in a format you control rather than
making an unknown string the format. A name that includes
a percent sign is just a name as a data argument, but a
percent sign in the format can select a conversion. Try
printing \cmd|'50% draft.txt'| as data with the fixed
format. The result keeps the percent sign as written even
though your lab contains no file by that name.

Ask ``who had to understand that notation?'' The terminal
app presents what you typed. The shell recognizes the quotes
and prepares the argument list. The utility interprets
\cmd|-n| and the count. The file lookup uses the path.
A correct path doesn't repair an invalid count, and a
supported count doesn't locate a misspelled file. Reading
the diagnostic with those layers in mind is usually faster
than changing two things and trying the command again.
When you paste an example, watch for typographic quotation
marks. If the shell displays a continuation prompt because
it is waiting for a closing quote, cancel the incomplete
input with \KeyChord{Ctrl}{C} and enter the whole command
again. Don't add random punctuation at that prompt; it can
become part of the command you were trying to fix.

You can also deliberately change the comparison's reporting
behavior. \cmd|cmp -l schedule.txt ./-revised.txt| reports each
differing byte rather than just the first. The first column
is its position, counted from one. The next two columns give
the two byte values in octal, a base-eight notation. Expect
three differences because \texttt{one} and \texttt{two}
differ at all three positions in these files. The option
doesn't change which bytes are compared. If you want a
reader-friendly account of which \emph{lines} changed, use
the comparison tools in Chapter~\ref{ch:22}; byte positions
are useful for checking an exact copy but are an awkward
way to read a schedule.

\begin{dialoguebox}{Quiet but not certain}
\Brian{I compared two files and nothing happened. So they match?}
\Stephen{Plain \texttt{cmp} normally stays quiet when the files match.}
\Brian{But you told me to use \texttt{-s}. It stayed quiet when I changed a line.}
\Stephen{You're right. With that option the status reports the difference.}
\Brian{Then anything other than zero means they're different?}
\Stephen{Only one means different. A higher status points to trouble, such as a missing file.}
\Brian{So I need to check that I got an answer before I use it.}
\Stephen{Exactly. Ask for the status right after the comparison.}
\DialogueConclusion{Silence is not a status.}
\end{dialoguebox}

\section{Practice and leave the directory tidy}
A useful routine is to check the working directory, write the command
as a sentence (``compare these two paths quietly''), then read it as
individual arguments. Predict a result you can check before using any
result as the basis for a later action. In these exercises only the
practice files are used; the final cleanup applies to all three. Keep
the setup files until you have completed the exercises.

\begin{exercise}{Prove the boundary}
Print a bracketed argument list containing the draft filename and the
revised filename, without reading either file. Predict the number of
bracketed entries. Alter just the draft argument to demonstrate a
boundary mistake without doing anything to the real file.
\end{exercise}
\begin{solution}
Use \cmd|printf '[%s]\n' 'Saturday draft.txt' ./-revised.txt| for
two entries. Remove the quotes around the draft name and expect
three entries. Both calls only print to the terminal; there are no
extra files to clean up.
\end{solution}

\begin{exercise}{Make the distinction with status}
Quietly compare the original schedule with the revision. Then change
the second path to a nonexistent file and compare again. Print each
status without running another command first.
\end{exercise}
\begin{solution}
Use \cmd|cmp -s schedule.txt ./-revised.txt|, followed immediately
by \cmd|printf '%s\n' "$?"|. It prints one. Repeat with
\cmd|not-created.txt| instead, and the status is above one.
Neither comparison changes a file; no additional cleanup is needed.
\end{solution}

\begin{exercise}{Separate the option's value from the operand}
Read two lines of the file with an initial hyphen using a path
that needs no end-of-options marker. Before running the command,
identify the value belonging to its option.
\end{exercise}
\begin{solution}
Use \cmd|head -n 2 ./-revised.txt|. The value of \cmd|-n| is
\cmd|2| and the operand is \cmd|./-revised.txt|. The lines are
\texttt{Doors noon} and \texttt{Tea at two}. There is no output file.
\end{solution}

Check \cmd|pwd| against the path you recorded, and stop if they
differ. If you created an original of your own there, copy it
somewhere safe first. Remove only the named practice files. The
interactive \cmd|-i| flag to \cmd|rm| asks before removing each
file. Confirm only the expected names. \cmd|rmdir| removes an
empty directory; if it refuses, inspect what remains rather than
switching to recursive deletion.

\begin{terminal}
rm -i schedule.txt 'Saturday draft.txt' ./-revised.txt
cd ..
rmdir "$lab"
\end{terminal}

\begin{takeaways}
\item The shell supplies arguments; the utility assigns them roles.
\item Quotes protect an argument boundary; a path prefix can protect the interpretation of a filename.
\item Read the previous status immediately and interpret it using the correct command's manual.
\end{takeaways}

